        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          Gemini 6 (G-6) was scheduled to be launched before Gemini 7 and rendezvous with another unmanned target.  An Agena rocket had been modified for use as a target vehicle for the planned orbital rendezvous and docking tests, but the unmanned rocket exploded shortly after liftoff.  G-6 was immediately canceled  and rescheduled to follow the Gemini 7 into space and rendezvous with it.  This was the first ever close rendezvous of two manned spacecraft.  The G-6 crew, Commander Walter M. Schirra, Jr. and Pilot Thomas P. Stafford, stayed in orbit for only one day, enough to complete its mission objective to demonstrate the feasibility of orbital rendezvous. Therefore, little time was available for scientific experimentation.<P>Several types of photography experiments were conducted on G-6.  Synoptic Terrain Photography was devoted to the study of the Earth terrain, and produced numerous useful pictures usable for geology, geography or oceanography.  Synoptic Weather Photography documented many weather patterns and events for meteorological studies.  Another, Dim Light Photography, flew as an operational experiment only.<P>Continuous, real-time physiological information on all Gemini crewmembers was monitored via telemetered data and voice transmissions.  Data was provided by a two-lead electrocardiogram, an impedance pneumograph measuring respiration, an oral thermistor measuring body temperature, and a blood pressure measuring system.  All of this hardware, including signal conditioners, was mounted on a biosensor harness that fit under the astronauts' flight suits.  Additionally, some inflight film footage was utilized for postflight assessment of crew health.<P> The two manned spacecraft, G-6 and G-7, approached each other as close as one foot,  demonstrating the feasibility of orbital docking and rendezvous, a skill that would be a necessary step in the voyage to the moon.<END>
        </div>

